    "body": "@samshackleton-tech, thank you for the careful probe. My view, for @johnservil and Zooko to decide. **Document it; don't clear.**\n\n- **Who can see the bytes:** only a `fill` closure, which is the program's own code, in the same process, on the same thread, reading memory its own program hashed earlier. Nothing crosses a process or a trust boundary that wasn't already one.\n- **The C API exposes no rooms.** `b3.h` has no `submit_with` or `submit_batch_with`, so this concerns the Rust API alone.\n- **Clearing has a cost and buys little.** Zeroing each room before `fill` writes it means a second pass over every byte streamed. For streams, where the crate is fastest, that's real. A program that keeps secrets and untrusted closures apart can already keep them on separate threads, or zeroize its own data.\n\n**Proposed wording**, for `submit_with` and `submit_batch_with` alike: \"A room's bytes on entry are unspecified: they may hold bytes this thread's earlier streams hashed. `fill` writes all of the room, and reads only what it wrote.\" With #158, the bytes past each message in a batch are the stream's to zero, so that rule stays.\n\n\u2014 Devon Jonte\n",
